WEBVTT

00:00:00.634 --> 00:00:01.630
Hello everyone.

00:00:01.802 --> 00:00:05.124
Today, we are talking about the smallest part of a haircut,

00:00:05.074 --> 00:00:06.510
and yet the most visible:

00:00:06.733 --> 00:00:09.550
the fringe, and the pieces that frame the face.

00:00:10.059 --> 00:00:12.750
The fringe sits right above the client's eyes.

00:00:13.176 --> 00:00:16.270
Every morning in the mirror, it is the first thing she sees.

00:00:16.619 --> 00:00:21.310
A couple of millimeters off, a couple of millimeters too short, and she notices.

00:00:21.659 --> 00:00:24.110
And hair only grows about a centimeter a month.

00:00:24.139 --> 00:00:28.190
A fringe cut too short is something the client has to live with for a long time.

00:00:28.807 --> 00:00:31.554
First, let us name a few kinds of fringe.

00:00:31.978 --> 00:00:32.994
A blunt fringe:

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:37.794
thick, down to the eyebrows, cut in a straight line, crisp and bold.

00:00:38.282 --> 00:00:41.154
It needs enough hair, and it has to be trimmed quite often.

00:00:41.739 --> 00:00:42.834
A wispy fringe:

00:00:43.091 --> 00:00:47.234
only a little hair, soft ends, and you can see the forehead through it.

00:00:47.571 --> 00:00:51.074
It is gentle, and suits someone trying a fringe for the first time.

00:00:51.406 --> 00:00:52.674
And the curtain fringe:

00:00:52.882 --> 00:00:55.774
parted in the middle, shortest right in the center,

00:00:55.724 --> 00:00:58.114
getting gradually longer toward the sides,

00:00:58.303 --> 00:00:59.634
framing the cheekbones.

00:01:00.219 --> 00:01:03.534
This one is the easiest to grow out, because the longer it gets,

00:01:03.484 --> 00:01:06.034
the more it blends into the hair around the face.

00:01:06.621 --> 00:01:09.234
Every place and every school names them differently.

00:01:09.596 --> 00:01:12.994
What matters is that you and the client picture the same shape.

00:01:13.687 --> 00:01:17.400
The consultation for a fringe has to be more thorough than usual.

00:01:17.803 --> 00:01:21.080
Pull all the hair back, and look at the face and the forehead.

00:01:21.564 --> 00:01:25.080
Is the forehead high or low, wide or narrow?

00:01:25.425 --> 00:01:27.560
Is the face round, or long?

00:01:27.971 --> 00:01:29.560
Then ask about her lifestyle.

00:01:29.899 --> 00:01:33.960
A fringe needs blow-drying every morning, even if only for a few minutes.

00:01:34.224 --> 00:01:35.320
Does she have the time?

00:01:35.669 --> 00:01:38.840
Does she wear glasses, or play sports and sweat a lot?

00:01:39.015 --> 00:01:40.680
Does she often tie her hair up?

00:01:40.846 --> 00:01:41.960
And say beforehand:

00:01:42.309 --> 00:01:44.600
a fringe needs a trim after a few weeks.

00:01:44.948 --> 00:01:47.246
If the client doesn't want to come back often,

00:01:47.196 --> 00:01:50.520
a long curtain fringe will be easier to live with than a blunt one.

00:01:51.117 --> 00:01:55.558
What new stylists forget most often is the direction of growth at the front hairline.

00:01:55.982 --> 00:01:58.678
Lift the hair, and look really closely.

00:01:59.095 --> 00:02:00.758
A lot of people have a cowlick there.

00:02:01.087 --> 00:02:03.478
Where there is a cowlick, the hair won't lie down.

00:02:03.973 --> 00:02:07.344
Cut the fringe short, and it stands straight up, or splits in two,

00:02:07.294 --> 00:02:09.558
and the client fights with it every morning.

00:02:09.816 --> 00:02:13.183
With a strong cowlick, leave the fringe longer and heavier,

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:14.758
so the weight pulls it down.

00:02:15.221 --> 00:02:18.838
Or choose a curtain fringe, parted in the direction the hair grows.

00:02:19.294 --> 00:02:21.478
Sometimes, the best advice is:

00:02:21.645 --> 00:02:22.598
don't get a fringe.

00:02:23.237 --> 00:02:27.322
Agree on the length while the hair is dry, and right on the client's face.

00:02:27.909 --> 00:02:30.561
Use your finger to lay the hair on the forehead,

00:02:30.511 --> 00:02:33.162
and point to exactly where the fringe will stop:

00:02:33.511 --> 00:02:37.482
touching the eyebrows, below the eyebrows, or at the cheekbones.

00:02:38.071 --> 00:02:40.122
Then cut it longer than that first.

00:02:40.525 --> 00:02:44.442
The first time, cut only to the tip of the nose, or to the lips.

00:02:44.879 --> 00:02:48.042
Only then go gradually shorter, a little at a time.

00:02:48.221 --> 00:02:52.442
You have heard this many times, but it is never truer than with a fringe:

00:02:52.649 --> 00:02:56.442
you can always cut more, but hair cut too short can't be put back.

00:02:57.083 --> 00:02:59.823
The section for a fringe is usually a triangle.

00:03:00.253 --> 00:03:04.463
The tip of the triangle is on top of the head, where the head starts to curve down.

00:03:04.945 --> 00:03:07.747
The bottom corners are at the corners of the forehead,

00:03:07.697 --> 00:03:09.583
just above the ends of the eyebrows.

00:03:10.076 --> 00:03:14.143
A deeper triangle, reaching further back, gives a thick fringe.

00:03:14.403 --> 00:03:16.783
A shallow triangle gives a wispy fringe.

00:03:17.021 --> 00:03:19.498
Don't take hair too wide toward the temples,

00:03:19.448 --> 00:03:22.863
or the fringe will cover the whole face, and look very heavy.

00:03:23.204 --> 00:03:25.875
Comb the rest of the hair back, and clip it neatly,

00:03:25.825 --> 00:03:27.983
so it doesn't get mixed in while you cut.

00:03:28.529 --> 00:03:30.400
Now, the most important thing:

00:03:30.727 --> 00:03:32.560
wet hair, and tension.

00:03:32.975 --> 00:03:35.600
Wet hair stretches longer and hangs heavier.

00:03:35.861 --> 00:03:40.880
Once it is dry, it shrinks back, and where there is a cowlick, it springs up too.

00:03:41.538 --> 00:03:45.165
If you pull the fringe down tight and cut, then when you let go,

00:03:45.115 --> 00:03:47.680
the fringe jumps up higher than you expected,

00:03:47.866 --> 00:03:49.600
sometimes by a whole centimeter.

00:03:49.874 --> 00:03:52.320
So a fringe is cut with almost no tension.

00:03:52.503 --> 00:03:55.725
Comb the hair down, let it rest on the forehead by itself,

00:03:55.675 --> 00:03:58.240
and just support it lightly with your fingers.

00:03:58.477 --> 00:04:02.595
Many stylists cut fringes dry, or at least blow-dry before the final cut,

00:04:02.545 --> 00:04:04.880
to see exactly where the fringe will sit.

00:04:05.504 --> 00:04:08.969
To soften the ends of a fringe, stylists point cut.

00:04:09.479 --> 00:04:13.429
Turn the scissor tips up, and cut very small snips into the ends,

00:04:13.379 --> 00:04:15.529
instead of cutting straight across.

00:04:16.118 --> 00:04:19.609
A blunt fringe gets only a little point cutting, to keep its line.

00:04:20.113 --> 00:04:24.809
Wispy and curtain fringes get more, so the ends are light, and fall naturally.

00:04:25.225 --> 00:04:25.929
But remember:

00:04:26.119 --> 00:04:30.489
point cut too deep, and the fringe looks choppy, and gets much thinner.

00:04:31.096 --> 00:04:34.464
With a curtain fringe, once you have the length in the center,

00:04:34.414 --> 00:04:35.695
part the fringe in two.

00:04:36.163 --> 00:04:39.619
Each half is cut on a diagonal, from the middle of the forehead,

00:04:39.569 --> 00:04:41.535
getting longer out to the cheekbone.

00:04:42.122 --> 00:04:46.217
Then the pieces around the face continue that diagonal, down to the chin,

00:04:46.167 --> 00:04:47.215
or the collarbone.

00:04:47.801 --> 00:04:52.040
Cut them outward, away from the face, so the hair falls to the sides,

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:53.375
and opens the face up.

00:04:53.618 --> 00:04:58.015
The fringe and the face framing must be one continuous line, with no break.

00:04:58.566 --> 00:05:01.251
Always check the balance with the client's head straight,

00:05:01.201 --> 00:05:02.916
and the eyes looking straight ahead.

00:05:03.386 --> 00:05:08.356
If she tilts her head slightly, or looks down, the fringe immediately looks uneven.

00:05:08.917 --> 00:05:11.474
Hold both sides of the fringe at the same time,

00:05:11.424 --> 00:05:14.996
and compare them at the same landmark, for example the cheekbones.

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:19.156
Then step back, and look at the whole face, not just the fringe.

00:05:19.685 --> 00:05:20.455
And safety.

00:05:20.777 --> 00:05:23.335
The fringe is where you cut closest to the eyes.

00:05:23.588 --> 00:05:25.975
Ask the client to close her eyes while you cut.

00:05:26.403 --> 00:05:29.815
Your fingers always stay between the blade and the client's face.

00:05:30.235 --> 00:05:32.695
The scissor tips never point toward the eyes.

00:05:32.887 --> 00:05:36.535
When point cutting, the tips point up into the ends, or outward.

00:05:36.959 --> 00:05:40.487
If the client is about to sneeze, or wants to talk, stop,

00:05:40.437 --> 00:05:42.135
and lift the scissors away.

00:05:42.407 --> 00:05:46.435
When you are done, brush the loose hair off the face very gently,

00:05:46.385 --> 00:05:48.455
before the client opens her eyes.

00:05:49.024 --> 00:05:52.544
Finally, show the client how to blow-dry the fringe at home.

00:05:52.970 --> 00:05:57.584
Dry the fringe first, while it is still wet, because a fringe dries very fast.

00:05:57.933 --> 00:06:02.543
Point the dryer nozzle down, comb the fringe to the left, then to the right,

00:06:02.493 --> 00:06:04.224
and only then let it settle.

00:06:04.577 --> 00:06:07.824
And book the client in for a fringe trim after a few weeks.

00:06:08.252 --> 00:06:11.184
Many salons offer a quick fringe trim between cuts.

00:06:11.336 --> 00:06:15.264
What is in this lesson is common trade experience, for practice.

00:06:15.676 --> 00:06:18.864
Every country and every school has its own way of working.

00:06:19.285 --> 00:06:22.224
And now, please take the short quiz below the video.

00:06:22.481 --> 00:06:23.264
See you next time.
